            <ArticleTitle>Acute myocardial infarction in a patient with coronary artery aneurysm and Crohn's disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>400-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Crohn's disease is a chronic inflammatory disorder of unknown cause involving the gastrointestinal tract. Complications from the cardiovascular system seem to be uncommon in patients with Crohn's disease. We present a case of a 37-year-old man with a known history of Crohn's disease, who was admitted to our hospital with acute myocardial infarction. An aneurysm of a totally occluded circumflex coronary artery was revealed during the attempt at primary intervention. The artery was successfully opened and the aneurysm was sealed with the use of 2 covered stents.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
